When designing a VPC architecture, should a Load Balancer be placed in a public subnet or private subnet? Why? 

1.A public-facing Load Balancer (LB) should be placed in a public subnet, while internal load balancers can reside in private subnets. 

2. Public subnets allow the LB to receive incoming traffic directly from the internet via an Internet Gateway and route it to backend servers located securely in private subnets. 

3.Placing LBs in the public subnet creates a dedicated "DMZ" (demilitarized zone) layer, while the application servers and databases remain protected in private subnets without public IPs. 

4.Only public-facing LBs require public subnets. Internal-only applications should use private subnets for improved security. 
 
5.A public load balancer can handle SSL/TLS termination and provide an additional layer of security, such as integration with a Web Application Firewall (WAF), before traffic reaches your internal services. 
